CCLuaJIT patch 07: interruptible C pattern matching (and table.sort, plain find)
Gate: -DLUAJIT_ENABLE_INTERRUPT (needs patch 01). Applies on top of 01-06.

- lib_string.c: match() (every backtracking step), the max_expand and
  matchbalance scan loops poll G(L)->intr (the flag set by luaJIT_interrupt()).
  lib_table.c: table.sort's partition loops poll it too (adversarial inputs
  make the quicksort O(n^2) without any Lua code running).
- lj_dispatch.c: lj_dispatch_intrpoll() runs the installed count hook from
  inside the C function, in a protected C frame (lj_vm_cpcall): the hook can
  raise an error (re-thrown in the C function, like "pattern too complex";
  xpcall handlers are skipped as for other hook errors, patch 02), cannot
  yield (lua_isyieldable() == 0, lua_yield() raises an error), may call
  lua_sethook/luaJIT_interrupt, run the GC and grow the Lua stack.
  A hook that defers a request re-arms it with luaJIT_interrupt(); the next
  LJ_INTR_CSKIP (4096) polls from C are then skipped. Never runs on a trace.
- lj_str.c: lj_str_find() (plain find; also called from JIT-compiled code and
  the recorder, where polling is impossible) switches from memchr/memcmp to
  the Two-Way algorithm after a work budget: worst case O(n+m) instead of
  O(n*m); typical searches are unchanged.

